Keep a page and the page it opened together, on a phone.

A phone keeps its tabs behind a grid. A link that opens a new one opens it
somewhere the reader cannot see, the tab count changes under them, and back
does not return to the page they came from, because the new tab has no
history of its own. Chrome and Brave on Android answer this by grouping the
two, and the shell draws the group as a strip of favicons beside the dock.

The engine decides membership and the shell decides how to draw it, so one
answer serves every window and survives a restart. Two fields carry it:
groupId, which is Chromium's own tab group, and openerId, which is the tab
that opened this one.

Grouping is added in AddWebContents, which is reached only when a page opens
another page -- the shell's own new tab, a restored tab and a bookmark go
elsewhere and none of them should start a group. Chromium already puts the new
page into the group the old one is in; what it does not do is start a group
when the old one is on its own, which on a phone is the usual case. The opener
goes in first, so the reader can get back to where they were from inside the
group rather than out of it. A window.open() carrying window features arrives
as NEW_POPUP and is left alone, because a page that asked for a window is
usually a login or a payment watching it.

Phones only, read from the UI family at the moment of the click, so an
unfolded foldable behaves like the tablet it has become and a folded one like
a phone. Groups made before a fold survive it; only what happens next changes.

The opener is kept beside TabStripModel's own rather than in it. The model
keeps one chain at a time -- inserting an active tab with ADD_INHERIT_OPENER
calls ForgetAllOpeners() first -- so opening a second page from the first
erases the first pair, and the shell draws every link. Chromium also does not
persist openers at all, so this one is written into the tab's session extra
data and translated back on restore, where the ids have changed.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.

diff --git a/chrome/browser/ui/browser_tabstrip.cc b/chrome/browser/ui/browser_tabstrip.cc
--- a/chrome/browser/ui/browser_tabstrip.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/browser_tabstrip.cc
@@ -3,6 +3,11 @@
 // found in the LICENSE file.
 
 #include "chrome/browser/ui/browser_tabstrip.h"
+
+#include "build/build_config.h"
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+#include "chrome/browser/ui/ohos/shell_tab_groups_ohos.h"
+#endif
 
 #include <optional>
 
@@ -121,7 +126,40 @@
 
   ConfigureTabGroupForNavigation(&params);
 
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+  // A phone hides its tabs behind a grid, so a page opening another page
+  // opens it where the reader cannot see it, and back does not return to
+  // where they came from. Keeping the two together is what Chrome and Brave
+  // do on Android, and the shell draws the pair as a strip of favicons
+  // beside the dock.
+  //
+  // ConfigureTabGroupForNavigation() above already puts the new page into the
+  // group the old one is in. What it does not do is start a group when the
+  // old one is on its own, which on a phone is the usual case -- so that is
+  // what this adds, with the opener first, so the reader can get back to the
+  // page they were on from inside the group.
+  //
+  // Only for a new tab. A window.open() carrying window features is a popup
+  // and arrives here as NEW_POPUP; it keeps its own window, because a page
+  // that asked for one is usually a login or a payment that is watching it.
+  if (!params.group &&
+      (disposition == WindowOpenDisposition::NEW_FOREGROUND_TAB ||
+       disposition == WindowOpenDisposition::NEW_BACKGROUND_TAB)) {
+    params.group = chrome::ohos::GroupForPageOpenedFrom(
+        browser->GetTabStripModel(), source_contents);
+  }
+#endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+
   Navigate(&params);
+
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+  // Who opened whom, kept beside TabStripModel's own answer rather than in
+  // it: the model keeps one chain at a time and forgets the rest whenever an
+  // active tab is inserted, and the shell draws every link.
+  chrome::ohos::RecordPageOpener(params.navigated_or_inserted_contents,
+                                 source_contents);
+#endif
+
   return params.navigated_or_inserted_contents;
 }
 
